      "message": {
        "reasoning_text": "Now I have a comprehensive view of all the changes. Let me now compile the notable changes and start building the release notes. \n\nLet me organize the key features:\n\n## Runtime / JIT\n- `Enable runtime-async for shared framework source projects in net11.0+` (#126343) - Major: runtime-async now enabled by default for net11.0 targets\n- `[RuntimeAsync] Remove [RequiresPreviewFeatures] on runtime async API` (#124488) - Runtime async no longer requires preview features\n- `Enable GC regions on macOS` (#125416) - GC feature\n- `Add runtime async support for saving and reusing continuation instances` (#125556)\n\n\n- Several follow-up runtime async improvements including optimizations for skipping unmutated locals in continuations, null handling in reused continuations, and NativeAOT support, plus enhancements for linux-riscv64\n- `Arm64: Add SM4 and SHA3 instructions` (#124504) - JIT hardware acceleration\n- `arm64: Fold negative variable eq/ne to 0` (#124332) - JIT optimization\n- `JIT: Convert multi-target switches to branchless checks` (#124567) - JIT optimization\n- `Improve HashSet<T> performance by enabling JIT bounds check elimination` (#125893) - Performance\n- `Improve Unwindable's performance` (#125545)\n- `Add InstructionSet_VectorT for ARM64` (#123992) - ARM64 vector support\n- `Implement SVE2 non-temporal scatter stores` (#123892) - ARM64 SVE2\n- `Add JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase and JsonKnownNamingPolicy.PascalCase` (#124644) - STJ feature\n- `Add JsonNamingPolicyAttribute for member-level naming policies in System.Text.Json` (#124645) - STJ feature\n- `Support type-level JsonIgnoreCondition via [JsonIgnore] on classes, structs, and interfaces` (#124646) - STJ feature\n- `Support generic converters on generic types with JsonConverterAttribute` (#123209) - STJ feature\n- `Support constructors with byref parameters (in/ref/out) in System.Text.Json` (#122950) - STJ feature\n- `Move Zstandard APIs from System.IO.Compression.Zstandard to System.IO.Compression` (#124634) - Zstandard in main namespace\n- `Add CRC32 validation when reading zip archive entries` (#124766) - Zip security\n- `TarWriter: when writing entries for the same hard-linked file use HardLink entries` (#123874)\n- `Synchronize PaxTarEntry ExtendedAttributes with property setters` (#123990)\n\nLooking at several more System.Text.Json enhancements around naming policies and converter flexibility, plus improvements to compression APIs including moving Zstandard to the main namespace and adding CRC32 validation for zip files, along with tar writer improvements for handling hard-linked files and extended attributes.\n\nThen there's work on relaxing RandomAccess type requirements for non-seekable files, allowing null arguments in Process APIs, vectorizing math operations like Asin and Adler32, handling special-use domain names in DNS resolution, optimizing I/O operations for pipes and zip extraction with preallocation, and fixing an IndexOutOfRangeException in FrozenHashTable.\n\nLooking at more improvements: fixing null checks in ImmutableArray builders, several regex optimizer enhancements to reduce backtracking and simplify the interpreter, stabilizing ML-KEM cryptography APIs, adding parameterized CRC variants, using OpenSSL for AES Key Wrap on Linux, and expanding hash algorithm support with SHA-384 and SHA-512.\n\nI'm also noticing some API changes like adding unsafe evolution attributes and ProcessStartOptions for platform-aware path resolution, though it looks like ProcessStartOptions was added then reverted, so that's not a net change to document.\n\nI see a couple of reverts in there too—one for forwarding activation signals and another for the loader heap backout adapter refactoring.\n\nFor Cryptography, the main highlights are ML-KEM graduating from experimental status, new parameterized CRC APIs, OpenSSL-based AES Key Wrap on Linux, and some COM interop improvements. ASP.NET Core is adding zstd Content-Encoding support alongside the existing Brotli compression.\n\nLooking at the rest of the ASP.NET Core changes, there's OpenAPI 3.2.0 support, variable-height item virtualization in Blazor, JSExport for events in Blazor WASM, and several HTTP/2 fixes addressing content-length mismatches with trailers, header payload padding, and Q/HPackDecoder validation. There's also a security fix for ManagedAuthenticatedEncryptor calculations.\n\nFor EF Core, the major additions include complex properties support in Cosmos with both querying and model building, partial property loading in relational queries, new ChangeTracker and migration APIs, and a breaking change that throws on missing migrations by default. There's also support for excluding foreign keys from migrations and using migration IDs as scaffolded type names.\n\nLooking at more EF Core improvements: adding a property to SharedTableConvention for unique keys across schemas, a parameterless overload for the pooled DbContext factory, changes to avoid loading vector properties during model building and change tracking, SQL optimizations for to-one joins, preserving batch terminators in migration scripts, and better exception handling when backing fields can't be found.\n\nFor Roslyn, there's automatic discovery for C# file-based apps, IDE completion support for include directives in those apps, and LSP TypeHierarchy support being added.\n\nNow looking at more C# and IDE work: CallHierarchy support in LSP, compilation caching improvements for the VBCSCompiler server, heuristic detection for file-based apps, regex search in NavigateTo, LSP selection range handling, updates to the unsafe evolution feature status, and including RequiresUnsafe in public API signatures.\n\nThere's also SDK work on target framework selection for multi-targeted projects and artifacts path support being added.\n\nContinuing with MSBuild improvements: preferring dotnet MSBuild.dll for out-of-proc workers in CLI scenarios for better performance, allowing virtual project references, fixing a ToolTask hang issue with grandchild processes, exposing TaskEnvironment construction APIs, and implementing thread-safe task analysis.\n\nAdding ETW events for node lifecycle tracking, fixing Unix SessionId handling for cross-terminal node reuse, significantly improving ItemSpecModifiers and BuiltInMetadata performance with benchmarks, resolving a macOS symlink handshake mismatch in the .NET task host, and fixing System.IO.File/Directory property functions when using relative paths in multi-threaded mode.\n\nFor NuGet, there's work on file-based app support in the XPlat CLI and a new security flag for untrusted root certificates in signing operations.\n\nNow looking at more NuGet fixes: addressing Central Package Management being ignored with the no-restore flag, improving NU1107 error messaging for transitive pinning scenarios, blocking path separators in aliases for security, and using aliases as a pivot point when listing packages.\n\nWinForms has a few targeted fixes including an InvalidCastException in Control.OnHandleDestroyed, a ComboBox collection copying issue, and infrastructure work to multi-target System.Private.Windows.Core.\n\nWPF is mostly infrastructure-focused with no significant user-facing changes.\n\nFor F#, there are several important bug fixes around type definition metadata, closure capture and let-rec initialization, method signatures with outref and static abstract byref parameters, and CLIEvent property recognition in the Symbol API, plus various regression tests.\n\nNow I'm ready to organize this into the release notes structure with a comprehensive changes file, a scored features subset, and component-specific markdown files. I'll start by creating the release branch and setting up the directory structure.\n\nLet me be strategic about scope and focus on the essential files: the build metadata, a complete changes list, a scored features file, and detailed markdown sections for the major components like libraries, runtime, ASP.NET Core, SDK, C#, and EF Core, with stubs for the rest.",
