            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# .NET Runtime in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\n.NET 11 Preview 3 includes updates to the runtime, GC, and JIT compiler:\\n\\n- [Runtime Async enabled by default for .NET 11](#runtime-async-enabled-by-default-for-net-11)\\n- [GC regions enabled on macOS](#gc-regions-enabled-on-macos)\\n- [ARM64: SM4 and SHA3 hardware intrinsics](#arm64-sm4-and-sha3-hardware-intrinsics)\\n\\n.NET Runtime updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [What's new in .NET 11 Runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-11/runtime) documentation\\n\\n## Runtime Async enabled by default for .NET 11\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nRuntime-async is now the default compilation model for all code targeting `net11.0` ([dotnet/runtime #126343](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126343)). Previously, opt-in was required. This means every async method you write against the `net11.0` TFM now uses the runtime-managed suspension model introduced in Preview 1.\\n\\nWith runtime-async, the compiler no longer generates state-machine classes for `async` methods. Instead, the runtime manages suspension and resumption directly. The benefits:\\n\\n- Cleaner stack traces: async calls appear as regular frames rather than compiler-generated state machine entries\\n- Lower allocation pressure: no state-machine heap objects per `async` invocation\\n- Better debuggability: the call stack reflects the logical code structure\\n\\nPreview 3 adds several optimizations that make runtime-async more efficient at scale:\\n\\n- **Continuation reuse** ([dotnet/runtime #125556](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125556)): the runtime now saves and reuses continuation instances across `await` points, reducing allocations in hot async loops\\n- **JIT: skip saving unmutated locals** ([dotnet/runtime #125615](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125615)): the JIT no longer writes locals that haven't changed into the continuation, reducing work per suspension\\n- **NativeAOT support** ([dotnet/runtime #123952](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123952)): the runtime-async feature switch is now enabled for NativeAOT's `System.Private.CoreLib`, so ahead-of-time compiled apps also benefit\\n\\nThe `[RequiresPreviewFeatures]` attribute was removed from the runtime-async API surface ([dotnet/runtime #124488](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124488)). You no longer need to annotate your project with `<EnablePreviewFeatures>` to consume the APIs.\\n\\nFor existing code that relies on the state-machine behavior (for example, code that inspects state-machine internals via reflection), a feature switch is available:\\n\\n```xml\\n<!-- Opt out of runtime-async for a specific project -->\\n<RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include=\\\"System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeAsync\\\"\\n    Value=\\\"false\\\"\\n    Trim=\\\"true\\\" />\\n```\\n\\nFor most apps no changes are needed. Preview feature behavior and APIs may evolve before the final .NET 11 release.\\n\\n## GC regions enabled on macOS\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nThe GC's region-based memory model is now enabled on macOS ([dotnet/runtime #125416](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125416)). This brings macOS into parity with Linux and Windows, which have used GC regions since .NET 6.\\n\\nGC regions divide the heap into smaller, independent units rather than one or two large segments. This allows the GC to return memory to the OS more promptly and gives the heap better locality. Server applications and tools running on macOS (including Apple Silicon) should see improved memory behavior over time.\\n\\nNo configuration changes are needed. GC regions activate automatically when the CLR starts on macOS.\\n\\n## ARM64: SM4 and SHA3 hardware intrinsics\\n\\nThe JIT now generates ARM64 SM4 and SHA3 instructions directly ([dotnet/runtime #124504](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124504)), enabling hardware-accelerated implementations on capable processors. This is exposed through the `System.Runtime.Intrinsics.Arm` namespace alongside the existing AES, SHA1, and SHA256 intrinsics.\\n\\nSM4 is a block cipher used in Chinese cryptographic standards (GB/T 32907-2016). SHA-3 (Keccak) hardware acceleration benefits post-quantum and modern hash scenarios.\\n\\n```csharp\\nif (Arm64.Sha3.IsSupported)\\n{\\n    // Use hardware-accelerated SHA3 on capable ARM64 hardware\\n    Vector128<ulong> result = Arm64.Sha3.Sha3msg1(v0, v1, v2);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Bug fixes\\n\\n- **JIT**\\n  - Fixed assertion failure during import for `GT_CNS_INT`/`GT_CNS_LNG` operations ([dotnet/runtime #126064](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126064))\\n  - Fixed multi-statement return block handling in `fgFoldCondToReturnBlock` ([dotnet/runtime #124642](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124642))\\n- **Threading**\\n  - Fixed reentrant Monitor wait deadlock ([dotnet/runtime #126071](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126071))\\n  - Fixed possible deadlocks on `PosixSignalRegistration` disposal on Windows ([dotnet/runtime #124893](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124893))\\n- **COM Interop**\\n  - Fixed stale RCW cache entries not being cleared when releasing wrapper objects ([dotnet/runtime #125754](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125754))\\n\\n<!-- Filtered features (significant engineering work, but too niche for release notes):\\n  - arm64 negative variable fold (#124332): JIT micro-optimization; user-invisible.\\n  - JIT multi-target switch to branchless (#124567): JIT codegen improvement; user-invisible.\\n  - Improve UnwindInfoTable performance (#125545): Internal JIT data structure; user-invisible.\\n  - cDAC APIs (multiple PRs): Internal diagnostics plumbing; no mainstream developer scenario.\\n  - WASM RyuJIT improvements (multiple PRs): Relevant only to WASM-targeting JIT contributors.\\n-->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/runtime.md\"}"
