              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# .NET Libraries in .NET 11 Preview 3 - Release Notes\\n\\n.NET 11 Preview 3 includes new .NET Libraries features & enhancements:\\n\\n- [System.Text.Json: PascalCase naming and more](#systemtextjson-pascalcase-naming-and-more)\\n- [ML-KEM API stabilization](#ml-kem-api-stabilization)\\n- [Zstandard moved to System.IO.Compression](#zstandard-moved-to-systemiocompression)\\n- [ZIP integrity: CRC32 validation on read](#zip-integrity-crc32-validation-on-read)\\n- [Parameterized CRC-32 and CRC-64](#parameterized-crc-32-and-crc-64)\\n- [DNS resolution honors RFC 6761 special-use domains](#dns-resolution-honors-rfc-6761-special-use-domains)\\n- [RandomAccess works with non-seekable streams](#randomaccess-works-with-non-seekable-streams)\\n- [Regex performance improvements](#regex-performance-improvements)\\n- [TensorPrimitives.Asin vectorized](#tensorprimitivesasin-vectorized)\\n\\n.NET Libraries updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [What's new in .NET 11 Libraries](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-11/libraries) documentation\\n\\n## System.Text.Json: PascalCase naming and more\\n\\nPreview 3 delivers a cluster of `System.Text.Json` serialization improvements spanning naming policies, generic converters, and constructor support.\\n\\n### PascalCase naming policy\\n\\n`JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` and the matching `JsonKnownNamingPolicy.PascalCase` enum value are now available ([dotnet/runtime #124644](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124644)). PascalCase is the .NET default for property names and is commonly required when serializing to APIs that expect Pascal-cased JSON.\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar options = new JsonSerializerOptions\\n{\\n    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase\\n};\\n\\nvar json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { firstName = \\\"Alice\\\", age = 30 }, options);\\n// Output: {\\\"FirstName\\\":\\\"Alice\\\",\\\"Age\\\":30}\\n```\\n\\n### Per-member naming policy with [JsonNamingPolicy]\\n\\nA new `[JsonNamingPolicy]` attribute lets you apply a naming policy to a specific property or field without affecting the rest of the type ([dotnet/runtime #124645](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124645)).\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic class Response\\n{\\n    [JsonNamingPolicy(JsonKnownNamingPolicy.CamelCase)]\\n    public string UserName { get; set; }  // serializes as \\\"userName\\\"\\n\\n    public string ProductId { get; set; }  // serializes as \\\"ProductId\\\" (class default)\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Type-level JsonIgnoreCondition\\n\\n`[JsonIgnore]` can now be applied at the class, struct, or interface level to set a default `JsonIgnoreCondition` for all members ([dotnet/runtime #124646](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124646)). This reduces boilerplate in DTO-heavy codebases.\\n\\n```csharp\\n[JsonIgnore(Condition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull)]\\npublic class UserDto\\n{\\n    public string Name { get; set; }\\n    public string? Email { get; set; }    // omitted when null\\n    public string? PhoneNumber { get; set; } // omitted when null\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Generic converters on generic types\\n\\n`[JsonConverterAttribute]` now supports generic converter types applied to generic classes ([dotnet/runtime #123209](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123209)). Previously, generic types had to register converters through `JsonSerializerOptions.Converters`.\\n\\n```csharp\\n[JsonConverter(typeof(MyGenericConverter<>))]\\npublic class Wrapper<T>\\n{\\n    public T Value { get; set; }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Constructors with byref parameters\\n\\n`System.Text.Json` can now deserialize into types whose constructors use `in`, `ref`, or `out` parameters ([dotnet/runtime #122950](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/122950)). This removes a limitation that required workarounds for immutable types with ref-parameter constructors.\\n\\n## ML-KEM API stabilization\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nThe `System.Security.Cryptography.MLKem` (ML-KEM, also known as CRYSTALS-Kyber) APIs have been stabilized and the `[Experimental]` attribute removed. ML-KEM is a NIST-standardized post-quantum key encapsulation mechanism (FIPS 203).\\n\\nYou can now take a production dependency on ML-KEM APIs without opting into experimental features:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Generate a new ML-KEM-768 key pair\\nusing MLKem key = MLKem.GenerateKey(MLKemAlgorithm.MLKem768);\\n\\n// Encapsulate: produces a shared secret and ciphertext\\nbyte[] ciphertext;\\nbyte[] sharedSecret = key.Encapsulate(out ciphertext);\\n\\n// Decapsulate: recover the shared secret from the ciphertext\\nbyte[] recoveredSecret = key.Decapsulate(ciphertext);\\n```\\n\\nPost-quantum feature behavior may still evolve before the final .NET 11 release.\\n\\n## Zstandard moved to System.IO.Compression\\n\\nZstandard compression APIs are now in the `System.IO.Compression` namespace alongside `GZipStream`, `BrotliStream`, and `DeflateStream` ([dotnet/runtime #124634](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124634)). The previous `System.IO.Compression.Zstandard` sub-namespace is being phased out.\\n\\n`ZstandardStream` now follows the same pattern as the other compression streams:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Compress\\nusing var output = new FileStream(\\\"data.zst\\\", FileMode.Create);\\nusing var compressor = new ZstandardStream(output, CompressionMode.Compress);\\nawait inputData.CopyToAsync(compressor);\\n\\n// Decompress\\nusing var input = new FileStream(\\\"data.zst\\\", FileMode.Open);\\nusing var decompressor = new ZstandardStream(input, CompressionMode.Decompress);\\nawait decompressor.CopyToAsync(destination);\\n```\\n\\nThis change pairs with ASP.NET Core's new zstd content-encoding support in this preview.\\n\\n## ZIP integrity: CRC32 validation on read\\n\\n`ZipArchive` now validates CRC32 checksums when reading zip archive entries ([dotnet/runtime #124766](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124766)). Previously, CRC32 validation was only performed during extraction to a file. With this change, reading entry data through `ZipArchiveEntry.Open()` also detects corruption.\\n\\nApplications that extract untrusted archives now get integrity checking without additional code:\\n\\n```csharp\\nusing var archive = ZipFile.OpenRead(\\\"package.zip\\\");\\nforeach (var entry in archive.Entries)\\n{\\n    using var stream = entry.Open();\\n    // CRC32 is now validated as data is read; throws IOException on mismatch\\n    await stream.CopyToAsync(destination);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Parameterized CRC-32 and CRC-64\\n\\nNew APIs in `System.IO.Hashing` support parameterized CRC configurations, enabling you to compute CRCs that match specific file formats, protocols, or legacy systems that use non-standard polynomial/seed values ([dotnet/runtime #124441](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124441)).\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Compute a CRC-32 using Castagnoli polynomial (used in iSCSI, ext4, etc.)\\nvar crc = new Crc32(polynomial: 0x1EDC6F41, initialValue: 0xFFFFFFFF, inputReflected: true, outputReflected: true, finalXorValue: 0xFFFFFFFF);\\ncrc.Append(data);\\nuint checksum = crc.GetCurrentHashAsUInt32();\\n```\\n\\n## DNS resolution honors RFC 6761 special-use domains\\n\\n`System.Net.Dns` now recognizes RFC 6761 special-use domain names (.local, .localhost, .test, .example, .invalid, and similar) and handles them without sending queries to public DNS servers ([dotnet/runtime #123076](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123076)). This matches browser and operating system behavior and prevents information leaks in development and testing scenarios.\\n\\nThe `.localhost` domain resolves to the loopback address without a DNS lookup:\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar addresses = await Dns.GetHostAddressesAsync(\\\"service.localhost\\\");\\n// Returns loopback (127.0.0.1 or ::1) — no DNS query sent\\n```\\n\\n## RandomAccess works with non-seekable streams\\n\\n`RandomAccess.Read` and `RandomAccess.Write` now accept `SafeFileHandle` objects backed by non-seekable files such as pipes and character devices ([dotnet/runtime #125512](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125512)). Previously, calling these methods on a non-seekable handle threw `NotSupportedException`. The methods use the appropriate underlying platform call (`ReadFile`/`WriteFile` without an offset on Windows) for non-seekable handles.\\n\\n## Regex performance improvements\\n\\nPreview 3 includes several improvements to Regex matching performance:\\n\\n- **Optimizer pass improvements** ([dotnet/runtime #125289](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125289)): the regex optimizer now investigates and applies additional transformation passes, reducing the size and complexity of compiled patterns\\n- **Reduced backtracking for greedy loops** ([dotnet/runtime #125636](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125636)): greedy loops followed by subsumed literals no longer generate unnecessary backtracking paths; patterns like `a+b` now run with fewer backtracks\\n- **Case-sensitive leading string heuristics** ([dotnet/runtime #124736](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124736)): the engine applies a frequency-based heuristic to pick the best leading string for case-sensitive patterns, improving initial match position finding\\n\\nThese changes are passive; no API changes or code updates are required.\\n\\n## TensorPrimitives.Asin vectorized\\n\\n`TensorPrimitives.Asin` now uses `Vector.Asin` hardware intrinsics ([dotnet/runtime #126052](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126052)), enabling SIMD acceleration for inverse-sine computation over large numeric tensors. This benefits ML and signal-processing workloads that apply trigonometric functions element-wise.\\n\\n## Bug fixes\\n\\n- **System.Collections**\\n  - Fixed `IndexOutOfRangeException` in `FrozenHashTable` when creating from very large collections ([dotnet/runtime #125555](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125555))\\n  - Fixed `ImmutableArray<T>.Builder.RemoveAll` not accepting a null predicate ([dotnet/runtime #125920](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125920))\\n- **System.Diagnostics.Process**\\n  - `ProcessStartInfo` constructor and `Process.Start` now accept `null` for the arguments parameter ([dotnet/runtime #126076](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126076))\\n- **System.IO**\\n  - Fixed `SafeFileHandle.CanSeek` performance regression ([dotnet/runtime #125807](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125807))\\n  - Fixed `CreateSubdirectory` failing for root directories ([dotnet/runtime #121906](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/121906))\\n  - `TarWriter` now uses `HardLink` entries when writing multiple entries for the same hard-linked file ([dotnet/runtime #123874](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123874))\\n  - `PaxTarEntry.ExtendedAttributes` now stays in sync with property setters ([dotnet/runtime #123990](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123990))\\n  - Tar now correctly identifies only junction/symlink reparse points as symlinks, not all reparse points ([dotnet/runtime #124753](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124753))\\n- **System.Net**\\n  - Fixed `Uri.GetHashCode` equality contract violation for `file:` URIs ([dotnet/runtime #124652](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124652))\\n  - Fixed `Uri.TryUnescapeDataString` throwing instead of returning false with small destination buffers ([dotnet/runtime #124655](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124655))\\n- **System.Security**\\n  - Fixed `FromBase64Transform.TransformFinalBlock` not resetting state consistently ([dotnet/runtime #124480](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124480))\\n  - Fixed OpenSSL initialization error handling to properly report failures ([dotnet/runtime #125324](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125324))\\n- **System.Threading.Channels**\\n  - Fixed `SingleConsumerUnboundedChannel.WaitToReadAsync` returning an exception instead of false when the channel is empty and completed ([dotnet/runtime #125745](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125745))\\n- **System.Xml**\\n  - Fixed out-of-memory condition in `BinHexDecoder`, `Base64Decoder`, and `XmlSchemaValidator` when throwing on large invalid input ([dotnet/runtime #125930](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125930))\\n\\n<!-- Filtered features (significant engineering work, but too niche for release notes):\\n  - Simple ComWrappers APIs (#120114): targets COM interop library authors, not typical developers.\\n  - LoggerMessage source generator ref readonly preservation (#124589): narrow tooling detail.\\n  - Process overlapped I/O pipes on Windows (#125643): internal implementation detail; no API change.\\n  - Zip PreallocationSize/async access (#125260): internal ZipArchiveEntry optimization; no API change.\\n  - HashSet<T> bounds-check JIT win (#125893): passive, user-invisible.\\n-->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/libraries.md\"}"
