              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# ASP.NET Core in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\n.NET 11 Preview 3 includes the following new ASP.NET Core features:\\n\\n- [OpenAPI 3.1 schema support](#openapi-31-schema-support)\\n- [Blazor Virtualize: variable row heights](#blazor-virtualize-variable-row-heights)\\n- [Zstd response compression](#zstd-response-compression)\\n- [Blazor WebAssembly JSExport for event callbacks](#blazor-webassembly-jsexport-for-event-callbacks)\\n\\nASP.NET Core updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [What's new in ASP.NET Core 11](https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/release-notes/aspnetcore-11.0)\\n\\n## OpenAPI 3.1 schema support\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nASP.NET Core's built-in OpenAPI document generation now targets **OpenAPI 3.1** (which aligns with JSON Schema 2020-12) ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65415](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65415)). OpenAPI 3.1 is the current specification version and is supported by Swagger UI 5+, Scalar, and most modern tooling.\\n\\nKey differences from OpenAPI 3.0 that you get automatically:\\n\\n- Nullable types now use `type: [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]` rather than `nullable: true`\\n- `$schema` is emitted at the document level\\n- `summary` and `description` are properly separated in the generated output\\n\\nTo ensure tooling compatibility, verify that your OpenAPI viewer or code generator supports 3.1. The Scalar and Swagger UI packages bundled with ASP.NET Core have been updated accordingly. This is a preview feature and the schema generation behavior may change.\\n\\n## Blazor Virtualize: variable row heights\\n\\nThe Blazor `<Virtualize>` component now supports rendering items that have different heights ([dotnet/aspnetcore #64964](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/64964)). Previously, `Virtualize` required all items to have an equal, fixed height (specified via `ItemSize`). With this change, items can render at their natural height.\\n\\nUse the new `ItemSizingMode` parameter:\\n\\n```razor\\n<Virtualize Items=\\\"@items\\\"\\n            ItemSizingMode=\\\"ItemSizingMode.MeasureAllItems\\\">\\n    <ItemContent>\\n        <div class=\\\"item\\\">@context.Name</div>\\n    </ItemContent>\\n</Virtualize>\\n```\\n\\n`MeasureAllItems` measures each item's height after the first render, then uses those measured heights for subsequent scroll calculations. This works well for lists where heights differ but are stable once rendered (for example, text cards, comment threads, or product tiles).\\n\\nThe original `ItemSizingMode.FixedSize` behavior is preserved as the default for scenarios where all items have a known, uniform height — it is more efficient when applicable.\\n\\n## Zstd response compression\\n\\nASP.NET Core's response compression middleware now supports Zstandard (zstd) content encoding ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65479](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65479)). Zstd typically achieves better compression ratios than gzip at similar CPU cost, and modern browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) accept `Content-Encoding: zstd`.\\n\\nTo enable:\\n\\n```csharp\\nbuilder.Services.AddResponseCompression(options =>\\n{\\n    options.Providers.Add<ZstandardCompressionProvider>();\\n    options.EnableForHttps = true;\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThe compression level and negotiation behavior follow the same pattern as the existing Brotli and Gzip providers.\\n\\n## Blazor WebAssembly JSExport for event callbacks\\n\\nBlazor WebAssembly apps can now export .NET methods as JavaScript callable functions using `[JSExport]`, and wire those methods directly as DOM event listeners ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65897](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65897)). This enables tighter interop when JavaScript code needs to call back into Blazor without going through the full JSInvokable mechanism.\\n\\n```csharp\\n[JSExport]\\npublic static void OnButtonClick(string eventType, string targetId)\\n{\\n    // called directly from JavaScript\\n    Console.WriteLine($\\\"Received {eventType} from {targetId}\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\n// JavaScript side\\nconst handler = Module.exports.MyApp.OnButtonClick;\\ndocument.getElementById(\\\"myBtn\\\").addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", (e) =>\\n    handler(e.type, e.target.id));\\n```\\n\\n## Bug fixes\\n\\n- **Data Protection**\\n  - Fixed `IDataProtectionProvider` sometimes returning `null` in certain DI container configurations ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65890](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65890))\\n- **HTTP/2**\\n  - Fixed `ConnectionResetException` being thrown instead of completing the response when a client resets a stream ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65765](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65765))\\n  - Fixed incorrect flow-control accounting when a DATA frame arrives after a stream completes ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65771](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65771))\\n  - Fixed server not sending `GOAWAY` when a client aborts the connection prematurely ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65729](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65729))\\n\\n## Community contributors\\n\\nThank you to the community contributors who contributed to ASP.NET Core in .NET 11 Preview 3:\\n\\n<!-- TODO: verify community contributor list from aspnetcore git log -->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/aspnetcore.md\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
