            "id": "tooluse_UPzy4QPetauQ0i6PVxyG2a",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# EF Core & Data in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\n.NET 11 Preview 3 includes the following new EF Core features:\\n\\n- [Cosmos DB: complex property querying](#cosmos-db-complex-property-querying)\\n- [Partial property loading](#partial-property-loading)\\n- [ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState()](#changetrackergetentriesforstate)\\n- [Migration improvements](#migration-improvements)\\n- [Parameterless AddPooledDbContextFactory](#parameterless-addpooleddbcontextfactory)\\n\\nEF Core updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [What's new in EF Core 11](https://learn.microsoft.com/ef/core/what-is-new/)\\n\\n## Cosmos DB: complex property querying\\n\\nEF Core now supports querying complex properties from Azure Cosmos DB ([dotnet/efcore #37577](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37577)) and configuring complex property models ([dotnet/efcore #37919](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37919)). Complex properties are owned value types embedded directly in a document rather than as a separate entity with its own identity.\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Model\\npublic class Order\\n{\\n    public int Id { get; set; }\\n    public Address ShippingAddress { get; set; }  // complex property\\n}\\n\\npublic class Address  // no Id — value type embedded in document\\n{\\n    public string Street { get; set; }\\n    public string City { get; set; }\\n}\\n\\n// Query\\nvar orders = await context.Orders\\n    .Where(o => o.ShippingAddress.City == \\\"Seattle\\\")\\n    .ToListAsync();\\n```\\n\\nConfiguration follows the same fluent API as relational complex properties:\\n\\n```csharp\\nmodelBuilder.Entity<Order>()\\n    .ComplexProperty(o => o.ShippingAddress);\\n```\\n\\n## Partial property loading\\n\\nEF Core 11 Preview 3 adds partial property loading, allowing you to load only a subset of scalar columns or navigation properties into an entity after it has already been materialized ([dotnet/efcore #37857](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37857), [dotnet/efcore #37829](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37829)).\\n\\nThis is useful when an entity was loaded with `AsNoTracking()` or via a projection, and you later need a property that wasn't included in the initial query:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Initially loaded without the large BlobData column\\nvar product = await context.Products\\n    .Select(p => new Product { Id = p.Id, Name = p.Name })\\n    .FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);\\n\\n// Later, load only BlobData when needed\\nawait context.Entry(product).Property(p => p.BlobData).LoadAsync();\\n```\\n\\n## ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState()\\n\\nA new `ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState(EntityState state)` method returns all tracked entities in a given state as an efficient `IEnumerable<EntityEntry>` ([dotnet/efcore #37847](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37847)). Previously, filtering by state required calling `Entries()` and applying a LINQ `Where`, which allocated an intermediate list.\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Get all modified entities without full allocation\\nforeach (var entry in context.ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState(EntityState.Modified))\\n{\\n    Console.WriteLine(entry.Entity.GetType().Name);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Migration improvements\\n\\nPreview 3 includes several targeted improvements to EF Core migrations:\\n\\n### Exclude foreign keys from migrations\\n\\nA foreign key constraint can now be excluded from migration generation while still being tracked by the model ([dotnet/efcore #37815](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37815)). This is useful when the FK relationship is enforced by another mechanism (for example, by the application layer) and you want EF to use the relationship for queries without creating the database constraint.\\n\\n```csharp\\nmodelBuilder.Entity<Order>()\\n    .HasOne(o => o.Customer)\\n    .WithMany(c => c.Orders)\\n    .HasForeignKey(o => o.CustomerId)\\n    .HasConstraintName(null);  // Excludes from migration\\n```\\n\\n### Migration ID uses type name\\n\\nMigration file IDs now use the migration class's short type name as a suffix ([dotnet/efcore #37841](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37841)). This produces shorter, more predictable migration file names that are easier to read in source control.\\n\\n### Configurable SQL batch terminators\\n\\n`DbContextOptionsBuilder` now accepts a custom SQL batch terminator to control the separator between SQL statements in generated scripts ([dotnet/efcore #37810](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37810)). This is valuable when targeting databases or tools that require a non-default terminator (such as `GO` in SQL Server Management Studio scripts).\\n\\n## Parameterless AddPooledDbContextFactory\\n\\n> Community contribution\\n\\n`AddPooledDbContextFactory<TContext>()` can now be called without explicit configuration options ([dotnet/efcore #37144](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37144)). This simplifies registration in scenarios where the `DbContext` reads its connection string from other DI-registered configuration (for example, `IConfiguration`).\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Before: required explicit lambda\\nservices.AddPooledDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>(opts =>\\n    opts.UseSqlServer(connectionString));\\n\\n// After: configuration can come from the DbContext constructor\\nservices.AddPooledDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>();\\n```\\n\\nThank you to [community contributor](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37144) for this contribution.\\n\\n## Breaking changes\\n\\n### MigrationsNotFoundException is now thrown when no migrations are found\\n\\n`MigrationsNotFound` now throws `MigrationsNotFoundException` instead of silently returning an empty result when no migrations are found in the configured assembly ([dotnet/efcore #37839](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37839)). This affects calls to `context.Database.GetMigrations()` and `context.Database.GetPendingMigrations()` when the migrations assembly contains no migration types.\\n\\n**Workaround**: If you intentionally start with no migrations, use a try/catch or check for empty results before calling these APIs.\\n\\n## Bug fixes\\n\\n- Fixed `TransactionScopeAsync` not being properly cleaned up when the outer ambient transaction is rolled back ([dotnet/efcore #37834](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37834))\\n- Fixed `DbContext.SaveChanges` incorrectly marking detached entities as added in certain cascade scenarios ([dotnet/efcore #37807](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37807))\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/efcore.md\"}"
