              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# MSBuild in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\n.NET 11 Preview 3 includes the following MSBuild updates:\\n\\n- [Prefer dotnet MSBuild for out-of-proc task workers](#prefer-dotnet-msbuild-for-out-of-proc-task-workers)\\n- [Unix: cross-terminal MSBuild node reuse](#unix-cross-terminal-msbuild-node-reuse)\\n\\nMSBuild updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [MSBuild release notes](https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/releases)\\n\\n## Prefer dotnet MSBuild for out-of-proc task workers\\n\\nMSBuild now defaults to using `dotnet` (the CoreCLR host) instead of the full .NET Framework `MSBuild.exe` when spawning out-of-proc task worker nodes on Windows ([dotnet/msbuild #13452](https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/pull/13452)). This means task workers share the same runtime as the build driver, reducing cross-runtime marshaling and startup costs for large builds that parallelize task execution.\\n\\nProjects with tasks that require .NET Framework can opt back in by setting `MSBuildRuntimeType=Full` on the task definition.\\n\\n## Unix: cross-terminal MSBuild node reuse\\n\\nMSBuild nodes launched in one terminal session can now be reused by builds initiated from other terminal sessions on Unix ([dotnet/msbuild #13354](https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/pull/13354)). Previously, each terminal created its own isolated set of worker nodes because of differences in how process groups are handled. With this fix, node pools are shared across the user session, reducing startup latency for iterative builds and CI pipelines that start fresh shells between invocations.\\n\\n## Bug fixes\\n\\n- Fixed `ToolTask`-based tasks occasionally hanging when standard output and standard error buffers filled simultaneously ([dotnet/msbuild #13351](https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/pull/13351))\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/msbuild.md\"}"
            }
